{% extends "base.html.twig" %}

{% block title %}Getting Started{% endblock %}
{% block description %}Get started with Waaseyaa: install, migrate, create content, and explore the admin API.{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
<section class="page-header">
    <h1>Getting Started</h1>
    <p>From zero to a running Waaseyaa application with content and an admin API.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>PHP 8.4+</strong> with extensions: <code>pdo_sqlite</code>, <code>mbstring</code>, <code>json</code>, <code>openssl</code></li>
        <li><strong>Composer 2.x</strong> (<a href="https://getcomposer.org">getcomposer.org</a>)</li>
        <li><strong>SQLite 3</strong> (the default database)</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Create a New Project</h2>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">composer create-project waaseyaa/skeleton my-app
cd my-app</code></pre>
    <p>That scaffolds a full Waaseyaa application from the skeleton. Your project now has config files, a CLI binary, templates, and directory stubs for entities, controllers, and service providers.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Install and Migrate the Database</h2>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">php bin/waaseyaa migrate</code></pre>
    <p>This creates a SQLite database at <code>waaseyaa.sqlite</code> in your project root and runs all pending migrations. The skeleton ships with entity types for nodes, taxonomy terms, media, menus, and users. All of their tables are created here.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Start the Dev Server</h2>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">php -S localhost:8080 -t public</code></pre>
    <p>Open <a href="http://localhost:8080">http://localhost:8080</a> in your browser. You should see the Waaseyaa welcome page.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Explore What's Included</h2>
    <p>The skeleton ships with several entity types out of the box. List them:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">php bin/waaseyaa entity-type:list</code></pre>
    <p>You'll see <code>node</code>, <code>taxonomy_term</code>, <code>media</code>, <code>menu</code>, <code>user</code>, and others. These are the content building blocks that come with the framework packages.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Create Your First Content</h2>
    <p>Create a node entity from the CLI:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">php bin/waaseyaa entity:create node</code></pre>
    <p>The CLI walks you through setting field values interactively. Once created, verify it exists:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">php bin/waaseyaa entity:list node</code></pre>
    <p>Your node is stored in SQLite and immediately available through the JSON:API.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Hit the JSON:API</h2>
    <p>Every entity type gets a full CRUD API automatically. With the dev server running:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash">curl http://localhost:8080/api/node | python3 -m json.tool</code></pre>
    <p>That returns your node as a spec-compliant JSON:API response. Filtering, sorting, pagination, and relationship includes all work out of the box.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Access the Admin API</h2>
    <p>The admin surface package exposes API endpoints for building admin interfaces. These are available at <code>/admin/surface/</code>:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Get the admin catalog (what entity types are manageable)
curl http://localhost:8080/admin/surface/catalog | python3 -m json.tool

# List nodes through the admin API
curl http://localhost:8080/admin/surface/node | python3 -m json.tool

# Get a specific entity
curl http://localhost:8080/admin/surface/node/1 | python3 -m json.tool</code></pre>
    <p>The catalog endpoint returns a schema describing every entity type, its fields, and available actions. Admin UIs consume this to render forms and list views dynamically.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>Project Structure</h2>
    <pre><code>my-app/
├── bin/
│   └── waaseyaa              # CLI binary
├── config/
│   ├── waaseyaa.php          # Framework configuration
│   ├── entity-types.php      # Custom entity type registration
│   └── services.php          # Service overrides
├── public/
│   └── index.php             # Front controller
├── src/
│   ├── Controller/           # HTTP controllers
│   ├── Entity/               # Your entity classes
│   ├── Provider/             # Service providers
│   └── ...                   # Access, Domain, Seed, etc.
├── templates/                # Twig templates
├── storage/                  # Runtime storage
└── waaseyaa.sqlite           # SQLite database (after migrate)</code></pre>
    <p>Configuration lives in <code>config/</code>. Your application code goes under <code>src/</code>. The front controller at <code>public/index.php</code> boots the HTTP kernel. The CLI binary at <code>bin/waaseyaa</code> boots the console kernel.</p>
</section>

<section class="content-section">
    <h2>What's Next</h2>
